 the East Building) lies 28 m south and 6 m west of where it was drawn, at the same angle, so everything moved onto it; the trees that then fell off the plate were dropped; the Palace of Fine Arts is now the pale Bedford limestone it is (it was drawn in a darker stone), and the East Building's polished-aggregate concrete a dark blue-grey rather than near-black, so its coffered roof reads. Still guessed: column counts, wing heights, the vault's size; Art Hill's slope is not modelled."}